WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.427 --> 00:00:01.260
(glove snaps)

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.383
(phone rings)

00:00:04.383 --> 00:00:05.900
- Thanks for calling Mahogany Books.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:06.770
How can I help you?

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:10.170
- [Jason] For Mahogany Books
in Southeast Washington DC,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.580
this is what business unusual looks like.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.240
Orders taken by phone.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.073
- Yes ma'am.

00:00:16.073 --> 00:00:18.230
- [Jason] Books sanitized
before being hand-delivered

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:19.783
to customers parked out front.

00:00:20.721 --> 00:00:22.963
- Oh, thank you so much.

00:00:22.963 --> 00:00:24.660
- [Jason] Derrick and Ramunda Young

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.530
quit their full-time jobs years ago

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:30.300
to pursue their dream,
creating Mahogany Books,

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.270
a home for readers seeking literature

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.830
on the African American experience.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.970
- The biggest premise was how do we make

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.169
black books accessible
no matter where you live.

00:00:39.169 --> 00:00:41.740
I'm from Oklahoma, proud
to be from Oklahoma,

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.160
but I didn't have that type
of access to black books.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.680
- [Jason] When they founded
Mahogany Books 13 years ago,

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.980
it was an online bookstore.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.130
Nearly three years ago, they opened this,

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.826
their brick and mortar business.

00:00:53.826 --> 00:00:55.850
The Youngs say that it quickly grew

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.300
to account for 75% of their revenue.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.013
Then coronavirus hit.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.850
- Everything had stopped and
we had to shut our doors.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:06.530
It was a little scary
in the very beginning

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.645
because this was our bread and butter.

00:01:08.645 --> 00:01:10.980
- [Jason] African American
small business owners

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.450
have been disproportionately
affected by the pandemic.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.030
Because the Youngs had already
built their online business,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.359
they didn't have to shut down entirely,

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:22.330
but nationwide, from February
to April of this year,

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.390
the number of active African American

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.390
small business owners dropped 41%

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.750
compared to 22% of all
active small business owners.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:32.610
- We have to be wise.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.310
We still have to look at funding

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.370
that comes in different areas

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.945
because now, with doors closed,

00:01:36.945 --> 00:01:39.333
less traffic is coming in our space.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.160
- [Jason] When the Federal
Paycheck Protection Program,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.540
or PPP, passed as part of
the CARES Act in late March.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.775
- The motion is adopted.

00:01:47.775 --> 00:01:48.810
(politicians cheer)
(gavel pounds)

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.240
- [Jason] The Youngs
applied to the program

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.332
through their bank.

00:01:51.332 --> 00:01:54.100
- Before I was able to figure it out,

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.250
the first allotment of
money had already run out.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.240
It takes a little bit more

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.620
for, I think, genuinely small businesses

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.450
to figure out how to
navigate some of these things

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.140
because that's not necessarily
what our wheelhouse is.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.440
- [Jason] The Youngs experienced this

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.910
like many other African
American small businesses

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:12.943
trying to take advantage of PPP.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.160
- Knowing the history
of financial exclusion

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.640
means that we have a
program that was already

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.100
not gonna work to the best advantage

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.120
of business owned by people of color.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.010
- Ashley Harrington is a policy expert

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.110
at the Center for Responsible Lending,

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.730
a non-profit consumer advocacy group.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.530
So these programs go through the SBA,

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.290
the Small Business Administration.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.190
- They go through the SBA,

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.220
but the intermediary is
a financial institution.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.150
- Has this posed challenges
to African American businesses

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.160
more so than business owners at large?

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.210
- It absolutely has, right.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.690
So any time we have a program
that relies so heavily

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.430
on traditional financial institutions,

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:53.940
we're automatically gonna have a problem

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.630
for businesses owned by people of color,

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.080
particularly businesses
owned by black people,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.540
to get access to the funds

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.710
because they have a different
type of relationship

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.048
with major financial institutions
than other communities.

00:03:05.048 --> 00:03:07.730
They have typically not been served well

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.300
by the financial mainstream.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.220
- [Jason] The SBA says it
and the Treasury Department

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.240
value all lenders and are
actively engaged in outreach

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.850
to Community Development
Financial Institutions

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:19.187
and Minority Depository Institutions.

00:03:19.187 --> 00:03:22.777
"CDFIs and MDIs," it says,
"have traditionally excelled

00:03:22.777 --> 00:03:24.427
"in providing financial assistance

00:03:24.427 --> 00:03:28.700
"to minority communities and
underserved populations."

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.770
The Youngs say that, when
they launched their business,

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.140
they didn't rely on the banking system.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.850
- We didn't go to banks
and get approved for loans.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.310
We had to bootstrap it ourselves

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:39.770
and pull out of our 401ks that we had

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.580
when we were working and go to friends.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:43.810
- [Jason] So when the
Youngs didn't hear back

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.340
about their PPP application,
and they still haven't,

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.380
it's been weeks, they embraced the same

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.250
entrepreneurial spirit that they had

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.102
when they first opened their business.

00:03:52.102 --> 00:03:56.060
They moved in-person author
talks to virtual sessions.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.090
- To have a conversation
about where black America is.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.320
- [Jason] They participated
in DC's curbside pickup

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.500
pilot program.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.740
And they created book bundles
for parents homeschooling.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.230
And now, following the
killing of George Floyd,

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.300
the Youngs say there's
been a growing demand

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:11.470
for their books.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.750
They say they've seen a 400% increase

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.420
in online sales over the previous year.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.090
Since closing their store to customers,

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.590
the Youngs say revenue
has dropped only 10%.

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:23.790
- Even though online has
been rockin' and rollin',

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.350
we don't know how long this new space,

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.320
this new era's gonna be here, right.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.850
So even though today it's great,

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:32.390
I'm assuming that, after a while,

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:33.740
the online will kind of drop off,

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.040
and so that's what we're looking at

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.370
with grants and different
funding in that way.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:38.680
(paper rustles)

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.260
- [Jason] To help bridge the gap

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.470
and support their three
part-time employees,

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.180
they also applied for and were granted

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:47.900
an Economic Injury Disaster Loan, or EIDL,

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.440
through the Small Business Administration.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.127
EIDLs of up to $10,000 were
designed, the SBA says,

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.277
"to provide economic relief to businesses

00:04:56.277 --> 00:04:57.847
"that are currently experiencing

00:04:57.847 --> 00:05:00.080
"a temporary loss of revenue."

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.830
They don't have to be repaid.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.690
- So we can have it shipped
out with the other book.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:06.350
No, you're valid.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.180
- [Jason] They know part of
their value to the community

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.770
is in their curation.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.940
- Our logo is our daughter reading a book.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.950
- [Jason] And helping parents
pass on their heritage

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:16.090
to their children.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.300
- Having the online space be there

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.390
has been the lifesaver for us

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:22.250
because people can still shop,

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.400
people can still get the
resources they need at home

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.210
as they're homeschooling their kids,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.190
especially if they're looking
at culturally-sensitive

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.240
and culturally-aware books and resources.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.140
They now have a spot that
they can still do that

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:33.560
no matter where they live.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.580
- Experts say the net effect
of black-owned small businesses

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.422
facing disproportionate challenges

00:05:38.422 --> 00:05:42.170
could persist well beyond
the COVID pandemic.

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:44.240
So could what we've been discussing today

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.660
have an impact on the
wealth disparity in America?

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:49.568
- Absolutely.

00:05:49.568 --> 00:05:53.230
The important thing to remember
is that business ownership

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:56.010
had an incredible impact
on wealth creation, right.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.408
Business owners of color
were more likely to have

00:05:59.408 --> 00:06:01.810
a lot more wealth than people of color

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:03.060
who don't own businesses.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.120
So business ownership
is definitely one way

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.630
to help people of color build wealth

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.570
and get on the track to
closing the wealth gap.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.340
And so when we have a situation like this

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.600
where they can't get access
to a critical lifeline

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.580
that will help them stay
afloat and stay open,

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:18.020
they are definitely losing wealth

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.040
and that has a ripple effect
in their entire community.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.210
- [Jason] The Youngs may be doing better

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.970
than other minority businesses
in their community right now,

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.860
perhaps because they're
able to offer something

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.550
of intangible value, escape and knowledge

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.940
at a time of immense hardship.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.273
- So the total is $18.02.

00:06:35.273 --> 00:06:37.060
Thank you for supporting us as well.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.389
It means a lot, yeah.

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:40.889
(soft music)

